  "slug": "polarization-analyzing-0ae7",
  "summary": "Preprints pair mode-matching, topology optimization, and susceptibility bounds to specify metasurface polarization response.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is Jones-level control of polarization in metasurfaces and metagratings, pursued along two routes. arXiv:2611.27311 uses analytical mode-matching to design dielectric-loaded perforated metallic metagratings that achieve dual-polarized anomalous refraction, validated by full-wave simulation. arXiv:2611.28364 presents a differentiable topology optimization framework for polarization-analyzing gratings that removes the paraxial restriction of the Jones-Fourier formalism. arXiv:2611.34146 instead derives analytically tractable minimum effective-susceptibility bounds for realizing unitary and nonunitary Jones polarization transformations. The three items do not share a single methodology; the shared element is the attempt to specify polarization response either through numerical inverse design or through analytical limits on material response. What is new is the extension beyond paraxial treatments and the explicit bounding of achievable polarization transformations.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-28"
}
